Claude is describing a cardinality estimation error problem. This is probably the most common reason why PostgreSQL chooses a hash join or merge join when a nested loop would have been much faster.

The key idea is:

The optimizer does not choose join algorithms based on actual row counts. It chooses based on estimated row counts.

If the estimates are wrong, the join method can be catastrophically wrong.

When Nested Loop Is Better

Nested loop is best when:

Outer side = very small
Inner side = can be probed efficiently by index


Example:

SELECT *
FROM orders o
JOIN customers c
     ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id
WHERE o.order_id IN (1,2,3,4,5);


Assume:

orders filtered rows = 5
customers rows        = 100 million
customers.customer_id indexed


Actual work:

5 index lookups


Nested loop:

5 × index seek


Maybe only 50 page reads.

Hash join:

Read whole customers table
Build hash table


Millions of page reads.

Nested loop wins by enormous margin.

What Happens When Statistics Are Wrong

Suppose PostgreSQL estimates:

orders filtered rows
estimated = 500,000
actual    = 5


Optimizer thinks:

500,000 index lookups


That sounds expensive.

So it chooses:

Hash Join


because optimizer believes reading customers once is cheaper than 500,000 index lookups.

But reality:

actual = 5 rows


Nested loop would have been massively faster.

This is the classic case.

Example of Correlated Predicates

Suppose table:

sales
(
    country,
    state,
    city
)


Data:

California always belongs to USA
Ontario always belongs to Canada


Query:

SELECT *
FROM sales
WHERE country='USA'
AND state='California';


Optimizer may assume:

country selectivity = 10%
state selectivity   = 5%

result estimate = 0.5%


Reality:

all California rows are USA

actual = 5%


10x estimation error.

When this error propagates through multiple joins:

A
JOIN B
JOIN C
JOIN D
JOIN E


the final estimate can be off by:

100x
1000x
10000x


and the join method becomes completely wrong.

Why Big UNION ALL Queries Often Trigger This

You mentioned:

3000-line query with many UNION ALL branches

This is a very common place for bad estimates.

Imagine:

SELECT ...
FROM A JOIN B ...

UNION ALL

SELECT ...
FROM A JOIN B ...

UNION ALL

...


Suppose there are 100 branches.

Planner often estimates each branch separately then combines estimates.

A small error in every branch becomes huge.

For example:

estimated branch rows = 10,000
actual branch rows    = 10


Across 100 branches:

planner estimate = 1,000,000
actual           = 1,000


Now planner believes very large joins are happening and prefers:

Hash Join
Merge Join


while actual workload is:

many tiny index probes


which strongly favors nested loop.

I've seen exactly this in reporting systems and generated SQL.

A Scenario Where Hash Join Is Terrible

Tables:

fact_sales      = 1 billion rows
customers       = 100 million rows


Query:

SELECT *
FROM customers c
JOIN fact_sales s
 ON c.customer_id=s.customer_id
WHERE c.customer_id=12345;


Actual:

customer rows = 1
sales rows    = 500


Nested loop:

1 customer row
→ index lookup into fact_sales
→ 500 rows


Very cheap.

Bad estimate:

customers = 1 million rows


Planner chooses hash join.

Hash join now:

Scan all customers
Scan all sales
Build hash table


Hundreds of GB processed.

Runtime explodes.

A Scenario Where Merge Join Is Terrible

Merge join requires ordered inputs.

Suppose:

SELECT *
FROM A
JOIN B USING(id)
WHERE A.id = 100;


Actual:

A rows = 1
B rows = 10


Nested loop:

1 index probe into B


Almost free.

If planner believes:

A rows = 10 million
B rows = 20 million


Merge join might do:

Sort A
Sort B
Merge


Huge unnecessary sorting.

Nested loop would finish before the sort completes.

Why Forcing Nested Loop Sometimes Gives 4x Improvement

Your observation:

enable_hashjoin=off
enable_mergejoin=off


gives

4x faster


strongly suggests:

Situation 1 (most likely)

Planner thinks:

join output is huge


Reality:

join output is small


Therefore nested loops with indexes are better.

Situation 2

Planner underestimates index usefulness.

Common in:

partitioned tables
UNION ALL views
parameterized queries
correlated predicates
skewed data

Situation 3

Generic plan problem

If you're using prepared statements:

PREPARE ...
EXECUTE ...


or application frameworks,

PostgreSQL may generate a generic plan.

The generic plan doesn't know actual parameter values.

Example:

WHERE customer_id = :id


Planner assumes average customer.

For some customers:

10 rows


For others:

10 million rows


Generic plan picks one join strategy for everyone.

Often hash join wins in average costing, while nested loop wins for specific executions.

This is one of the most common reasons people see:

custom plan = very fast
generic plan = much slower

Rule of Thumb

Think of it like this:

Nested Loop

Best when:

Outer rows are small
Indexes exist
Highly selective predicates

Hash Join

Best when:

Large joins
Most rows participate
No useful indexes

Merge Join

Best when:

Large joins
Inputs already sorted
Range joins or large equality joins


For your specific case (3000-line UNION ALL query, nested loop 4x faster than generic plan), my first suspicion would be:

Cardinality estimation error
        +
Generic plan
        +
Many UNION ALL branches
        +
Highly selective predicates


The planner likely thinks millions of rows will flow through the joins, so it picks hash/merge joins, while the actual execution only touches a tiny fraction of rows and nested loop with index lookups wins. Looking at an EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) where estimated rows differ dramatically from actual rows (10x, 100x, or more) would usually confirm this immediately.

That's actually a very good question. Many database engineers ask the same thing:

"If the WHERE clause is highly selective, why doesn't PostgreSQL automatically realize Nested Loop is better?"

The answer is:

PostgreSQL absolutely tries to do that.

The problem is not the join algorithm itself.

The problem is that the planner often cannot accurately predict how selective the WHERE clause really is.

Let's walk through why.

What the planner really sees

Suppose you write:

SELECT *
FROM orders o
JOIN customers c
  ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id
WHERE o.region = 'US'
  AND o.order_date = '2026-08-31';


You know:

Actual rows matching:
region='US'
AND order_date='2026-08-31'

= 5 rows


But PostgreSQL doesn't know that.

It must estimate before execution.

Maybe statistics say:

region='US'        = 40%
order_date=...     = 10%


Then planner assumes independence:

40% × 10% = 4%


If table has:

100M rows


it estimates:

4M rows


instead of:

actual = 5 rows


Once planner believes 4 million rows will participate, Nested Loop becomes expensive in the cost model.

Why doesn't PostgreSQL just execute the WHERE clause first?

A common misconception is:

Apply WHERE first
See only 5 rows
Then choose Nested Loop


Unfortunately SQL optimization doesn't work that way.

To know there are only 5 rows:

WHERE region='US'
AND order_date='2026-08-31'


PostgreSQL would have to actually execute part of the query.

But planning happens before execution.

Otherwise every query would require:

1. Partially run query
2. Gather actual cardinalities
3. Re-plan
4. Run query


which is often more expensive than simply planning once.

Why does it work correctly most of the time?

Because most statistics are "good enough."

Example:

WHERE customer_id = 123


on a unique index.

Statistics know:

1 row


very accurately.

Planner chooses:

Index Scan
Nested Loop


and everybody is happy.

That's why most queries get reasonable plans.

The bad cases are special cases.

The real enemy: correlations

Imagine:

WHERE country='US'
  AND state='CA'


Actual data:

Every California row is in US.


Planner typically estimates:

country='US'   = 50%
state='CA'     = 10%

estimated = 5%


Reality:

10%


Already off by 2x.

Now add:

city='San Francisco'


Maybe off by 10x.

Then join five tables.

The error compounds.

Suddenly:

estimated = 10,000,000 rows
actual    = 100 rows


Now the planner starts preferring Hash Join.

Why hasn't PostgreSQL fixed this after 30 years?

Because it's an extremely hard problem.

Consider:

WHERE A = ?
AND B = ?
AND C = ?
AND D = ?


To perfectly estimate selectivity PostgreSQL would need statistics for:

A
B
C
D
A+B
A+C
A+D
B+C
B+D
C+D
A+B+C
A+B+D
...


The combinations explode.

For a large schema:

thousands
or
millions


of possible correlations.

Collecting and maintaining those statistics would make:

ANALYZE


enormously expensive.

So PostgreSQL uses approximations.

Why is UNION ALL especially difficult?

You mentioned:

3000-line query
many UNION ALL branches


This is one of the hardest estimation scenarios.

Imagine:

SELECT ...
WHERE customer_id = 1

UNION ALL

SELECT ...
WHERE customer_id = 2

UNION ALL

...


100 branches.

Planner has to estimate:

branch #1
+
branch #2
+
...
+
branch #100


Then estimate joins above the union.

One small error per branch becomes a huge error at the top.

I've seen plans where:

estimated rows = 50 million
actual rows    = 500


because of this effect.

At that point Hash Join starts winning in the cost model even though Nested Loop is obviously better.

Generic plans make it worse

In your environment I actually suspect this may be the bigger issue.

Suppose:

WHERE account_id = $1


Some accounts:

10 rows


Others:

10 million rows


Generic plan cannot know which account will be passed.

It therefore plans for an "average" account.

Maybe average estimate is:

100,000 rows


Then Hash Join looks reasonable.

But when runtime value is:

account_id = 42


and only 20 rows exist,

Nested Loop would have been far better.

This is one of the most common causes of:

custom plan = fast
generic plan = slow

Why don't databases always choose Nested Loop just in case?

Because there are cases where Nested Loop is catastrophically bad.

Example:

SELECT *
FROM fact1
JOIN fact2 USING(id);


Both tables:

500 million rows


No highly selective filter.

Nested Loop would essentially do:

500M × 500M checks


or billions of index probes.

Hash Join may run in minutes.

Nested Loop may run for hours.

So PostgreSQL cannot simply bias toward Nested Loop.

A planner that always preferred Nested Loop would perform terribly for data warehouse workloads.

What your situation suggests

Given:

3000-line query
lots of UNION ALL
generic plan slower
forcing enable_hashjoin=off
forcing enable_mergejoin=off
about 4x improvement

My bet would be:

Planner estimate:
    hundreds of thousands or millions of rows

Reality:
    hundreds or thousands of rows


Look at EXPLAIN ANALYZE.

The smoking gun is usually something like:

Hash Join
  rows=5,000,000    <-- estimate
  actual rows=500


or

Nested Loop
  rows=100,000      <-- estimate
  actual rows=50


When I see estimate/actual errors of 100x to 10,000x in UNION-heavy queries, the wrong join choice almost always follows naturally. The join algorithm is usually not the root problem. The root problem is that the planner was given a bad estimate, and with the information it had, the hash join was actually the logical choice.
